## Proof

Let $M(r)=\max_{|z|=r}|f(z)|$ for $r\in(a,b)$, and set $m(r)=\log M(r)$. Hadamard's three-circle theorem asserts that $m(r)$ is a convex function of $\log r$ on $(a,b)$; equivalently, for every $a<r_1<r_2<r_3<b$,

$$
m(r_2)\le \lambda\, m(r_1)+(1-\lambda)\,m(r_3),\qquad 
\lambda=\frac{\log r_3-\log r_2}{\log r_3-\log r_1}. \tag{$\ast$}
$$

We prove that equality holds in $(\ast)$ (for all triples, i.e. $m$ is affine in $\log r$) **if and only if** $f(z)=cz^{k}$ for some $c\in\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$ and $k\in\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}$.

---

### 1. The inequality (with equality analysis)

Fix $r_1<r_2<r_3$ in $(a,b)$ and write $t_i=\log r_i$. The function
$$
h(z)=\alpha\log|z|+\beta,\qquad 
\alpha=\frac{m(r_3)-m(r_1)}{t_3-t_1},\quad \beta=m(r_1)-\alpha t_1
$$
is harmonic on the annulus $\{r_1\le |z|\le r_3\}$ (since $0\notin A(a,b)$, so $\log|z|$ is harmonic there). By construction $h\equiv m(r_1)$ on $|z|=r_1$ and $h\equiv m(r_3)$ on $|z|=r_3$.

The function $u(z)=\log|f(z)|$ is subharmonic on $A(a,b)$ (with value $-\infty$ at the zeros of $f$). On the boundary of the closed sub-annulus $\overline{\mathcal A}=\{r_1\le |z|\le r_3\}$ we have $u\le h$. By the comparison principle for subharmonic functions,
$$
u(z)\le h(z)\quad\text{for all }z\in\overline{\mathcal A}.
$$
Restricting to $|z|=r_2$ and taking the maximum gives
$$
m(r_2)\le \alpha t_2+\beta=\lambda\,m(r_1)+(1-\lambda)\,m(r_3),
$$
which is $(\ast)$.

---

### 2. Equality forces $f(z)=cz^k$

Suppose equality holds in $(\ast)$ for a triple $r_1<r_2<r_3$. Then
$$
m(r_2)=\alpha t_2+\beta=\max_{|z|=r_2}h(z).
$$
Since $u\le h$ on $\overline{\mathcal A}$ and $u(z_0)=h(z_0)$ for some $z_0$ with $|z_0|=r_2$ (an **interior** point of the open annulus $\{r_1<|z|<r_3\}$), the subharmonic function $v:=u-h\le 0$ attains its maximum $0$ at an interior point. By the strong maximum principle, $v\equiv 0$ on $\{r_1<|z|<r_3\}$, i.e.
$$
\log|f(z)|=\alpha\log|z|+\beta\quad\text{on }\{r_1<|z|<r_3\}. \tag{1}
$$

Being equal to the harmonic function $h$, the subharmonic function $\log|f|$ is harmonic on this sub-annulus; hence $f$ has **no zeros** there (at a zero $\log|f|=-\infty$). On any simply-connected subdomain $D\subset\{r_1<|z|<r_3\}$ choose a branch $\Log z$ and define $F(z)=f(z)\,e^{-\alpha\Log z}$, which is holomorphic on $D$. Equation (1) gives $|F(z)|=e^{\beta}$, a constant, on $D$; by the open mapping theorem $F$ is constant on $D$:
$$
f(z)=c\,z^{\alpha}\quad\text{on }D,\qquad |c|=e^{\beta}. \tag{2}
$$

For $f$ to be single-valued on the annulus, analytic continuation of (2) once around the origin must return the same value:
$$
c\,z^{\alpha}=c\,(e^{2\pi i}z)^{\alpha}=c\,e^{2\pi i\alpha}z^{\alpha}\;\Longrightarrow\;e^{2\pi i\alpha}=1\;\Longrightarrow\;\alpha\in\mathbb Z.
$$
Writing $\alpha=k\in\mathbb Z$, we obtain $f(z)=c\,z^{k}$ on the sub-annulus $\{r_1<|z|<r_3\}$, and by the identity theorem (both sides holomorphic on $A(a,b)$) this extends to **all** of $A(a,b)$:
$$
f(z)=c\,z^{k}\quad\text{on }A(a,b),\qquad c\ne 0,\;k\in\mathbb Z.
$$

Since $f$ is assumed non-constant, $k\ne 0$ (otherwise $f\equiv c$).

---

### 3. Converse

If $f(z)=c\,z^{k}$ with $c\ne 0$ and $k\in\mathbb Z$, then on $|z|=r$
$$
M(r)=\max_{|z|=r}|c|\,|z|^{k}=|c|\,r^{k}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad m(r)=\log|c|+k\log r,
$$
which is an **affine** (hence convex with equality everywhere) function of $\log r$. Thus equality holds in $(\ast)$ for every triple.

---

### Conclusion

Equality holds in Hadamard's three-circle theorem for a non-constant holomorphic $f$ on $A(a,b)$ if and only if
$$
\boxed{\,f(z)=c\,z^{k}\,}
$$
for some constant $c\in\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$ and some nonzero integer $k\in\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
